        -:   12:fasta2hash() and hash2fasta_file() used to be pure perl in ~/Scripts/bioinf.pm:
        -:   13:one readline, chomp and string append per line in, and one substr-sized SV per
        -:   14:80-column line out. Here a file is read in large blocks and every line of a
        -:   15:record is appended straight onto that record's SV, so no per-line SV is ever
        -:   16:made, and each finished sequence is shrunk to its length, which is the RAM a
        -:   17:proteome or genome actually needs rather than whatever the last buffer growth
        -:   18:left. On the file described at FASTA_BLOCK below, bioinf.pm read it in 3.83 s
        -:   19:with a peak RSS of 1,098 MB, and this in 0.80 s and 967 MB, 900 MB of which is
        -:   20:the sequences themselves; writing it back took 44.44 s and takes 0.97 s, and
        -:   21:the two files are byte-identical. The perl side (lib/Bioinf/Basic.pm) opens and
        -:   22:closes the files.
        -:   23:
        -:   24:Every croak() and warn() here starts with C99's __func__, so a message names
        -:   25:the C function that raised it (read_fasta or write_fasta) and stays right if
        -:   26:one is renamed.*/
