        -:   38:defline and its sequence can be COW copies of the one buffer -- so the
        -:   39:qualifier would be a false promise. perl is built with -fno-strict-aliasing,
        -:   40:where `restrict` is the only disambiguation there is, but it earns nothing even
        -:   41:on the buffer: the loop over it stores only through memchr() and sv_catpvn(),
        -:   42:which are opaque calls the compiler must assume write anywhere. The generated
        -:   43:code for this file was byte-identical with and without it (gcc -O2 -std=gnu99,
        -:   44:2026-09-30), so the one annotation records the contract rather than buying
        -:   45:speed.
